# The exact fork releases a MeshBench build fetches, pinned by tag.
#
# Sourced by .github/workflows/package.yml, and checked against
# internal/app/resource/toolchaincatalogue.go by TestPinsMatchTheCatalogue, so
# the emulator CI puts in a bundle and the emulator a first run downloads onto
# somebody's own machine can never be two different builds.
#
# The pipeline used to ask both forks for /latest. Anybody re-cutting a fork
# release then changed what every MeshBench build bundled with no commit here
# to show for it, which is how a QEMU release that renamed its assets and
# dropped every platform but Linux reached three platforms' bundles under a
# green pipeline. Moving a pin is now an edit with a diff.
#
# The rule a pin is held to:
#
#   - it names a tag, never "latest" and never a branch
#   - the tag's release is built from a named commit on the fork's
#     meshbench-main branch, and the tag's name matches that commit
#   - asset names are exact, because "the release exists and carries something
#     else" is only sayable when we know what was expected
#   - an empty asset is a platform the fork publishes no build for. The bundle
#     ships without that emulator, deliberately, and says so out loud
#   - whoever cuts a fork release moves the pin, in the same change as the
#     digests beside it in the catalogue
#
# Neither emulator pin below was chosen: both are what happened to be
# publishable on the day the runtime fetcher landed, and both want re-cutting
# from meshbench-main under a tag that names the commit it was built from.
# Until that happens they are at least fixed, which is the part that stops a
# bundle changing underneath a release.

# QEMU carries our SX1262 device and the GPIO work upstream has not got. This
# release publishes Linux x86_64 alone: the fork's own job builds only that,
# its macOS and Windows legs disabled for want of a runner, and the older
# multi-platform build predates the DIO1 wiring this version drives, so a board
# started against it dies asking for a machine property it has not got. An
# emulator that cannot boot a board is worse in a bundle than absent from one.
QEMU_RELEASE=meshbench-20260901-6b3a41a
QEMU_ASSET_LINUX_AMD64=qemu-meshbench-linux-x86_64.tar.gz
QEMU_ASSET_DARWIN_ARM64=
QEMU_ASSET_WINDOWS_AMD64=

# Renode carries the SEVONPEND fix, without which published nRF52 firmware
# sleeps for ever with its wake condition already true.
RENODE_RELEASE=meshbench-20260814-339f4df
RENODE_ASSET_LINUX_AMD64=renode-1.16.1.linux-portable-meshbench.tar.gz
RENODE_ASSET_DARWIN_ARM64=renode-meshbench-macos-arm64.tar.gz
RENODE_ASSET_WINDOWS_AMD64=meshbench-renode-1.16.1.windows-portable.zip

# The SX1262 model both emulators clock over a socket. Every emulated node
# needs it before either emulator, so a bundle without it ships two emulators
# nothing can drive.
RADIOSERVER_RELEASE=radioserver-v2
RADIOSERVER_ASSET_LINUX_AMD64=radioserver-linux-amd64
RADIOSERVER_ASSET_DARWIN_ARM64=radioserver-darwin-arm64
RADIOSERVER_ASSET_WINDOWS_AMD64=radioserver-windows-amd64.exe
